---
title: "Upgrade to v1.8"
description: "Breaking changes, migration steps and behavior changes when upgrading Outpost from v1.7 to v1.8."
---

This guide covers what changes when upgrading from v1.7 to v1.8. There is one breaking change, one ClickHouse migration and four behavior changes to review before upgrading.

With the ClickHouse log store, run `outpost migrate apply --yes` before starting v1.8: the server refuses to start while a migration is pending. There is no new PostgreSQL migration. Retries scheduled by v1.7 run normally on v1.8, and you can roll back to v1.7.0 without reverting the migration.

| Change | Impact | Action Required |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Webhook deliveries no longer follow redirects](#webhook-deliveries-no-longer-follow-redirects) | Webhook destinations whose URL answers with a redirect | Update the destination URL to the final address |

For new features and fixes, see the [v1.8.0 release notes](https://github.com/hookdeck/outpost/releases/tag/v1.8.0).

## Breaking Changes

### Webhook deliveries no longer follow redirects

A 3xx response is now a failed attempt with its status code, retried like any other failure and, when enabled, counted toward auto-disable. v1.7 followed up to 10 redirects. On a 301, 302 or 303 it re-sent the request as a `GET` without the body, so the new URL never received the event, yet the attempt was recorded as a success.

Common causes are an `http://` URL redirected to `https://` and a missing or extra trailing slash. The failed attempt's response data includes the redirect target in `location`, so you can see the URL to switch to. The `redirect_error` error code is removed, as it can no longer occur. See [Webhook](/docs/outpost/destinations/webhook#configuration).

## Behavior Changes

- With `CLICKHOUSE_LOG_RETENTION_TTL_DAYS` set, ClickHouse removes expired logs a whole part at a time instead of rewriting parts to delete expired rows. Tables are partitioned by month, so logs can stay queryable and on disk for up to about a month after their retention ends. With a short retention, plan for the extra disk.
- Hookdeck destinations retry a failed delivery. v1.7 recorded a non-2xx response from Hookdeck as failed but never retried it, and treated a 3xx as a success. Each retry is a new failed attempt, so a failing Hookdeck destination reaches consecutive failure alerts and auto-disable sooner, and can now send `alert.attempt.exhausted_retries`.
- A receiver that closes the connection before responding is recorded as `connection_reset`. v1.7 recorded it as `network_error`, which happened most often through a forward proxy (`DESTINATIONS_PROXY_URL`).
- With auto-disable off, the 100% [`alert.destination.consecutive_failure`](/docs/outpost/features/operator-events#alertdestinationconsecutive_failure) alert is sent once per failure streak. v1.7 sent it again on every failure past the threshold.

## New Configuration

See the [configuration reference](/docs/outpost/self-hosting/configuration) for defaults and details.

- [`DESTINATIONS_BLOCK_PRIVATE_ADDRESSES`](/docs/outpost/self-hosting/deployment#block-private-addresses-in-outpost) and `DESTINATIONS_PRIVATE_ADDRESS_ALLOWLIST`: stop webhook destinations from reaching private addresses. Off by default.
- [`CLICKHOUSE_IDLE_CLOSE_SECONDS`](/docs/outpost/self-hosting/configuration#log-storage): close the ClickHouse connection pool after this many seconds without a call (default `300`); the next call reconnects.
